{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Domain 2: Operational reliability","context":"## Trace requests across multi-agent workflows\n\nMulti-agent workflows hand requests between agents that delegate tasks and pass context. Without correlation, you can't tell which agent started a workflow, what context the agent passed, or where a chain of decisions went wrong.\n\n### Add correlation metadata to every agent\n\nTreat agent instrumentation like any production-distributed system. Include correlation metadata (agent_id, session_id, and trace_id) in structured logs. Propagate trace context through every inter-agent message. Watch for cascading decisions, where one agent's action triggers chains of downstream decisions that amplify errors.\n\n### Use AWS X-Ray and CloudWatch Transaction Search\n\n[AWS X-Ray](https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/xray-services-agentcore.html) provides distributed tracing for multi-agent systems. It uses the X-Amzn-Trace-Id header to propagate trace context across service boundaries and create a causal chain that links operations across agents. For agents on AgentCore Runtime, turn on CloudWatch Transaction Search once. Then, AgentCore Observability emits OpenTelemetry-compatible traces to CloudWatch automatically. You can view these traces through the Generative AI Observability dashboard or CloudWatch Transaction Search.\n\nFor agents implemented as Lambda functions, Amazon Elastic Container Service (Amazon ECS) tasks, or other compute services, instrument them with AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry (ADOT) to generate AWS X-Ray traces. Propagate the X-Amzn-Trace-Id header through all inter-agent messages: EventBridge events, Amazon Simple Queue Service (Amazon SQS) messages, Amazon Simple Notification Service (Amazon SNS) notifications, and API calls. Include correlation metadata in structured logs so that logs and traces tie together.\n\n### Correlate AWS DevOps Agent investigations with downstream MCP servers\n\nAWS DevOps Agent investigations that reach external systems through connected MCP servers create a tracing consideration. AWS DevOps Agent publishes vended logs and CloudWatch metrics for investigation activity, and the AWS DevOps Agent Journal captures MCP server calls. AWS DevOps Agent manages internal orchestration of MCP tool calls so you don't have to.\n\nOn the MCP server side, where you do have control, emit traces and structured logs so that each downstream call from the agent can have end-to-end correlation. Skills that direct AWS DevOps Agent to use specific MCP tools can document expected logging behavior so that you can trace investigation activity from the Agent Space to the resources that those tools touch","url":"https://repost.aws/articles/ARRrnN9FMVRve7wI2svOTiRg/domain-2-operational-reliability"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Build context-rich research agents with Deep Agents and Bedrock AgentCore | Artificial Intelligence","context":"## Trace and debug your agent\n\nAgentCore Observability gives you visibility into this multi-agent architecture through Amazon CloudWatch. AgentCore emits traces and spans in OpenTelemetry (OTEL) format, so you can view the full orchestration hierarchy on the CloudWatch GenAI Observability page: the coordinator’s run at the top level, a child span for each research subagent, and the analyst subagent that follows. Within each span, you can review tool calls with their inputs, outputs, timing, and token usage, confirm that the research subagents ran concurrently from their wall-clock timing, and identify which subagent and tool call encountered an issue when a browser navigation or code run does not succeed. As a one-time setup per account, you enable CloudWatch Transaction Search before traces and spans become available. When you host the agent on AgentCore Runtime (Part 2), the runtime instruments your agent with OTEL automatically, so no additional configuration is needed. To run the same agent outside the runtime, add the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry (ADOT) SDK and the LangChain instrumentation library to your agent. For more information, refer to the Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Observability documentation.\n\nYou can also score the quality of these traces with Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Evaluations, which provides built-in evaluators such as goal success rate and tool selection accuracy. For more details, refer to the AgentCore Evaluations documentation.\n\nIf you prefer, you can also use LangSmith for tracing. With LangSmith, you get end-to-end tracing that helps you debug this multi-agent architecture. Set three environment variables to turn on automatic tracing:\n\n```\nexport LANGCHAIN_TRACING_V2=true\nexport LANGCHAIN_API_KEY=\"your-langsmith-api-key\"\nexport LANGCHAIN_PROJECT=\"competitive-research-agent\"\n```\n\nThe accompanying notebook reviews this option","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-context-rich-research-agents-with-deep-agents-and-bedrock-agentcore/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore and AWS X-Ray","context":"# Amazon Bedrock AgentCore and AWS X-Ray\n\nAmazon Bedrock AgentCore integrates with AWS X-Ray to provide distributed tracing capabilities for your AI agents and tools. This integration allows you to track requests as they flow through your agent applications, helping you\nidentify performance bottlenecks and troubleshoot issues.\n\nAgentCore supports distributed tracing through X-Ray integration, allowing you to monitor the performance of your AI agents and tools. When you enable observability for your AgentCore resources, you can propagate trace context across service boundaries\nand gain visibility into how your agents interact with other AWS services. For more information, see Amazon Bedrock AgentCore.\n\nAgentCore supports the following X-Ray features:\n\n* Propagation of trace context to downstream services\n* Custom instrumentation using the AWS Distro for OpenTelemetry (ADOT) SDK","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/xray/latest/devguide/xray-services-agentcore.html"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Debugging production agents with Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Observability | Artificial Intelligence","context":"### Enabling observability\n\nBefore you begin debugging, enable CloudWatch Transaction Search for your account. This allows Amazon Bedrock AgentCore to send trace and metric data to CloudWatch. After you enable this setting, the service begins collecting observability data across your agents, memory systems, and tool integrations. You can then access this data through CloudWatch dashboards and Logs Insights queries","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/debugging-production-agents-with-amazon-bedrock-agentcore-observability/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"View observability data for your Amazon Bedrock AgentCore agents","context":"### Traces and Spans\n\nTraces provide visibility into request execution paths through your agent:\n\n* Location: the `spans` log stream in the agent’s log group (`/aws/bedrock-agentcore/runtimes/<agent_id>-<endpoint_name>`), or the `default` log stream in the `aws/spans` log group for agents that use the shared span destination. For more information, see Span destination for agents hosted in Amazon Bedrock AgentCore runtime.\n* Access via: CloudWatch Transaction Search console\n* Requirements: CloudWatch Transaction Search must be enabled\n\nTraces automatically capture:\n\n* Agent invocation sequences\n* Integration with framework components (LangChain, etc.)\n* LLM calls and responses\n* Tool invocations and results\n* Error paths and exceptions\n\nFor distributed tracing across services, you can use standard HTTP headers:\n\n* AWS X-Ray format: `X-Amzn-Trace-Id: Root=1-5759e988-bd862e3fe1be46a994272793;Parent=53995c3f42cd8ad8;Sampled=1`\n* W3C format: `traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01`\n\nTo view traces:\n\n* Navigate to CloudWatch console\n* Select **Transaction Search** from the left navigation\n* Filter by service name or other criteria\n* Select a trace to view the detailed execution graph","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/observability-view.html"}]}}

