Newer than your training data may be. When these conflict with the documentation results above or with what you remember, prefer the most recent dated source.

{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore now delivers unified observability with traces and logs in a single log group - AWS","context":"Amazon Bedrock AgentCore has updated its log delivery system, combining agent traces and prompts with logs in a single log group in Amazon CloudWatch. Previously, agent telemetry was split across multiple log groups, requiring users to search in multiple places for debugging. With this update, all of an agent's telemetry, including traces, prompts, structured logs, and standard output, is delivered to a single per-agent log group. This change allows for easier correlation of traces and logs, finer-grained access control, and customer-managed key (CMK) encryption at the individual agent level. Newly created agents in supported AWS regions starting July 20, 2026, will use unified observability by default. Existing agents can enable the feature by setting the `UNIFIED_TRACES_DESTINATION_ENABLED=true` environment variable and upgrading ADOT to version 0.17.1 or later. This feature is available in all AWS commercial regions where AgentCore runtime is supported","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/amazon-bedrock-agentcore-unified-observability-single-log-group/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"aws-observability","skill_description":"Investigates root causes on the Omni context graph (service dependencies); builds/configures/debugs AWS observability - operator-symptom questions, detecting Omni vs classic. CloudWatch on a reporting service: fleet health, Log Insights (cost), metric/composite/anomaly alarms, custom metrics/EMF, dashboards, X-Ray/ADOT tracing, canaries, CloudTrail (incl. OTLP/PromQL), CloudWatch Profiles (CPU/memory hotspots, flame graphs), Dynamic Instrumentation (live breakpoints), Application Signals service map. CloudWatch Omni on an existing Space: SQL over logs/traces, PromQL, LLM inference (vLLM) diagnosis, Omni dashboards, alerts, Slack notifications, programmatic/IaC access, driving Omni from coding agents/skills, AI agent quality from traces - on-demand/online evals incl. setup, readback, evaluators. First-time setup (Omni Space, access grants, ingestion, ADOT instrumentation for Application Signals/Omni): use setting-up-cloudwatch-observability. Not for app logging or threat detection.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, cloudwatch-omni, xray, cloudtrail, synthetics, application-signals\nTasks: build, deploy, debug, optimize, configure, enable, onboard, instrument, query\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability","skill_name":"aws-observability"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"setting-up-cloudwatch-observability","skill_description":"First-time setup for CloudWatch observability — Omni (CloudWatch Application Observability) and classic CloudWatch. Omni: creating a Space or Domain; access grants (who, at what level), access profiles for alerts/integrations/agents; instrumenting an app or AI agent with plain ADOT so traces reach Omni (Python/Node/Java/.NET on EC2/ECS/EKS/Lambda), incl. no-image-rebuild, CoreCLR; vLLM servers (AI Inference page); ingesting Azure or Google Cloud telemetry via CloudWatch agent (VM/AKS, GCE/GKE), Azure resource/VNet flow logs via CloudWatch Connector; connecting Slack or AWS DevOps Agent to a Space; whether GitHub or a custom MCP server can connect. CloudWatch: onboarding to Application Signals: ADOT auto-instrumentation, amazon-cloudwatch-observability add-on, monitored service, ServiceEvents, CI/CD metadata, Terraform. For using what is set up — queries, dashboards, alarms, Omni alerts, X-Ray, synthetics, Dynamic Instrumentation, AI agent evaluation, detecting Omni vs classic — use aws-observability.\n\nServices: cloudwatch, cloudwatch-omni, application-signals, iam\nTasks: onboard, configure, instrument\nPersona: developer, devops\nWorkload: observability","skill_name":"setting-up-cloudwatch-observability"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"amazon-bedrock","skill_description":"Builds generative AI applications on Amazon Bedrock. Covers model invocation (Converse API, InvokeModel), RAG with Knowledge Bases, Bedrock Agents, Guardrails, and AgentCore (including the Harness managed agent loop). Applies when invoking models, setting up or migrating Knowledge Bases to Managed, deploying to AgentCore, migrating/porting/converting a Bedrock Agent (including inline agents) to an AgentCore Harness, troubleshooting Bedrock errors (ThrottlingException, AccessDeniedException), or choosing Bedrock models (Claude, Llama, Nova, Titan). Also for prompt caching, quota and throttling diagnosis, cost tracking, migrating between Claude model generations, chunking strategies, and Bedrock model selection. Covers AgentCore Payments (x402, microtransactions, Payment Manager, Connector, Instrument, Coinbase CDP, Stripe Privy, paid endpoints, agent payments). NOT for custom model training, Rekognition, or Comprehend. For SageMaker model discovery, customization, or endpoints, use aws-ai-ml.\n\nServices: bedrock, bedrock-runtime, bedrock-mantle, bedrock-agent, bedrock-agent-runtime, bedrock-agentcore-control, bedrock-agentcore\nTasks: deploy, debug, optimize, design, migrate\nPersona: developer, devops, architect\nWorkload: generative-ai, rag, agents","skill_name":"amazon-bedrock"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Search AWS docs for AgentCore tracing setup"}
